# ============================== HR 5 ==============================
LESSONS_H5 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Performance management: scope and record structure", "11", "<p>This module covers performance management in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: build Appraisal Templates with weighted Key Result Areas and rating criteria; run an Appraisal Cycle that generates appraisals for its appraisees; track Goals through the period; process self appraisal and Employee Performance Feedback; read the final score computation; and run the cycle's completion and distribution reads.</p><p><b>The record structure.</b> Performance uses five record types:</p><p>1. <b>Appraisal Template</b> — the reusable definition of what a role is assessed on: the KRA table with weightages and the rating criteria (Chapter 2). Built once per role family.<br>2. <b>Appraisal Cycle</b> — the dated container for one assessment round: the period, the appraisee list, and the evaluation settings. Starting the cycle generates the Appraisals (Chapter 3).<br>3. <b>Appraisal</b> — one employee's assessment for one cycle: the KRA scores, the self appraisal, the feedback, and the final score (Chapters 4-6).<br>4. <b>Goal</b> — a tracked objective for an employee, linked to a KRA, carrying progress percentage; goals can nest as parent and child (Chapter 4).<br>5. <b>Employee Performance Feedback</b> — a reviewer's structured feedback against an appraisal, rated on the template's criteria (Chapter 5).</p><p><b>The boundary with pay.</b> Stated before anything is configured: the appraisal record <b>informs</b> pay and promotion decisions; it does not execute them. A pay change that follows an appraisal travels the Payroll track's lanes in full — the approval matrix, the letter, the Salary Structure Assignment (its module 6); a promotion travels module 7's lifecycle documents. The appraisal's final score is an input the deciders read, and the score's arithmetic (this module) must therefore be explainable: weightages recorded, ratings recorded, computation visible on the record.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> HR configures templates and runs cycles; employees maintain goals and submit self appraisals; the named reviewers submit feedback; the deciders decide. The performance policy — what is assessed, how scores are used, the cycle calendar — is the business's written policy, confirmed before configuration per the standing rule.</p><p><b>What reads performance records.</b></p><p>1. The pay review round (the Payroll track, module 6) — final scores as decision inputs.<br>2. The promotion and transfer documents (module 7) — the appraisal history on the employee.<br>3. The training-needs read (module 6 of this track) — low-scored KRAs as training-need signals.<br>4. The cycle reads (Chapter 7) — completion and distribution as management data.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Appraisal Template. Chapter 3: the Appraisal Cycle. Chapter 4: Goals and the KRA scores. Chapter 5: self appraisal and Employee Performance Feedback. Chapter 6: the final score. Chapter 7: the cycle reads and administration. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Appraisal Template", "12", "<p>Open Appraisal Template (awesome bar) &gt; New. One template per role family that is assessed on the same result areas. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Template Title.</b> Name the template for its role family: <i>Branch Retail Staff</i>, <i>Warehouse Operations</i>, <i>Management</i>.</p><p><b>2. The KRA table (Key Result Areas).</b> One row per result area the role is assessed on:</p><p>• <b>KRA</b> — the result area, from the KRA master (create the KRA records first: short names for assessable result areas — <i>Dispensing accuracy</i>, <i>Stock control</i>, <i>Customer service</i>, <i>Team supervision</i>).<br>• <b>Weightage (%)</b> — the KRA's share of the score. <b>The weightages must total 100</b>; the document validates the total. Set the weightages from the written performance policy: the weightage is the business's statement of what matters most in the role, and it is decided by the role's manager and the owner, not defaulted to equal splits without a decision.</p><p>Rules for the table: three to six KRAs per template (fewer than three assesses too little; more than six dilutes every weightage into noise); each KRA written as an observable result area, not a personality trait — the goal records (Chapter 4) must be able to attach measurable objectives to it.</p><p><b>3. The Rating Criteria table.</b> One row per criterion the reviewers rate on, with its weightage (also totalling 100 where the configuration weights them): the qualitative dimensions the feedback stage assesses — <i>Reliability</i>, <i>Communication</i>, <i>Policy compliance</i>. The criteria rows become the rating rows on every Employee Performance Feedback against appraisals using this template (Chapter 5) — the same structured-comparison design as the recruitment module's Expected Skill Set: every reviewer rates the same rows.</p><p><b>4. Save.</b> The template is reusable across cycles; a role family's template changes through the standing change discipline — a revised template applies to the next cycle, and past appraisals keep the template they were assessed on (history stands).</p><p><b>Template design procedure.</b></p><p>1. The role family's manager drafts the KRAs and weightages from the role's actual duties (the Designation's description — module 1 — is the starting document).<br>2. The owner confirms the weightages — the 100 points are a decision.<br>3. HR enters the template and records the decision reference on it.<br>4. The template is reviewed annually with the performance policy: KRAs that no longer match the role's duties are revised for the next cycle.</p><p><b>Verification.</b> Per template: weightages total 100 in both tables; every KRA exists in the KRA master and is observable; the criteria rows are ratable dimensions; the decision reference recorded.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Appraisal Cycle", "12", "<p>Open Appraisal Cycle (awesome bar) &gt; New. One cycle per assessment round. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Cycle Name, Start Date, End Date.</b> Name the cycle for its round — <i>2026 Annual</i>, <i>2026 H1 Probation Reviews</i> — and set the assessment period the cycle covers. Goals and feedback belong to the period; the final scores describe it.</p><p><b>2. Company and the appraisee list.</b> Select the company and build the appraisees table — the employees this cycle assesses. Use the selection tools (department, branch, designation filters) to load groups, then adjust by name. Each appraisee row carries the employee and the <b>Appraisal Template</b> that applies to them — the role-family mapping from Chapter 2 executed here: retail staff to the retail template, warehouse to warehouse, managers to management.</p><p><b>3. The KRA evaluation method.</b> The cycle-level setting that decides how KRA scores compute:</p><p>• <b>Automated based on goal progress</b> — each appraisal's KRA score derives from the progress of the Goals linked to that KRA (Chapter 4's mechanics). Use where goals are genuinely maintained through the period.<br>• <b>Manual rating</b> — the appraiser rates each KRA directly at assessment. Use where the business runs assessment-time rating without period-long goal tracking.</p><p>Choose per the written policy and the business's actual discipline — the honest-records rule from the attendance module applies: automated scoring over goals nobody updates produces scores nobody believes. Record the choice on the cycle.</p><p><b>4. The final score formula.</b> Where the configuration exposes the final score weighting (the shares of goal/KRA score, self appraisal, and reviewer feedback in the final figure — Chapter 6), confirm the settings against the policy before the cycle starts. The formula is part of the policy communication to staff: people being assessed are told how the number is built.</p><p><b>5. Create Appraisals.</b> Start the cycle: the system generates one Appraisal per appraisee, each carrying its template's KRA rows. Verify the count — appraisals generated equals appraisee rows — and spot-check three: the right template on the right role.</p><p><b>The cycle calendar.</b> Publish the round's dates when the cycle starts, per the written policy:</p><p>1. Cycle start — appraisals generated; goals confirmed (Chapter 4).<br>2. The period — goals updated as work progresses.<br>3. Assessment window opens — self appraisals due by a stated date; reviewer feedback due by a stated date.<br>4. Final scores computed and the calibration read (Chapter 7) run.<br>5. Outcome conversations held; decision inputs handed to the deciders (the Chapter 1 boundary).</p><p>The dates are the cycle's checklist; Chapter 7's completion read tracks submissions against them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Goals and the KRA scores", "12", "<p>Open Goal (awesome bar) &gt; New — or through employee self-service, the standard lane. Goals carry the period's objectives and, in automated cycles, compute the KRA scores.</p><p><b>1. The Goal record's fields.</b></p><p>• <b>Employee</b> — the goal's owner.<br>• <b>Goal name</b> — the objective, written as a verifiable outcome: <i>Reduce dispensing errors below two per month</i>, <i>Complete the monthly stock count by the 3rd</i>. A goal whose completion cannot be verified cannot honestly carry a progress figure.<br>• <b>KRA</b> — the template KRA this goal serves. The link is the scoring path: in automated cycles, a KRA's score derives from its linked goals' progress.<br>• <b>Appraisal Cycle</b> — the cycle the goal belongs to.<br>• <b>Start Date / End Date</b> — the goal's own span within the period.<br>• <b>Progress (%)</b> — the completion figure, updated by the employee as work advances.<br>• <b>Parent Goal</b> — goals nest: a parent objective with child goals, the parent's progress deriving from its children. Use nesting for large objectives with staged parts; keep the tree shallow (one level of children) or the structure costs more than it informs.</p><p><b>2. Goal setting, at cycle start.</b></p><p>1. The employee drafts goals per KRA — every template KRA carries at least one goal, or that KRA has no scoring basis in an automated cycle.<br>2. The manager confirms the goals: verifiable, within the role, weighted sensibly across the KRAs. The confirmation is recorded (the manager's comment on the goal or the site's approval step where configured).<br>3. Mid-period goal changes (a project cancelled, priorities moved by the business) are edits with the manager's confirmation noted — the standing documented-change rule, applied to objectives.</p><p><b>3. Progress discipline.</b></p><p>1. Progress updates are made when the work moves, not reconstructed in the assessment week — the month-end goal read (Chapter 7) lists stale goals (no update in the period's rhythm) as data to managers.<br>2. Progress states the verifiable position: the figure the evidence supports. The manager's confirmation at assessment (step 4 below) is the check.</p><p><b>4. The KRA score, computed.</b> In an automated cycle: each KRA's score = the progress of its linked goals (averaged or weighted per the configuration), scaled by the KRA's template weightage into the appraisal's goal score. At the assessment window the manager reviews the goals behind each KRA before the score stands — a 100% progress claim on an unverifiable goal is returned for evidence, exactly as any figure without a record is returned anywhere else in the programme.</p><p><b>5. Manual-rating cycles.</b> Where the cycle's method is manual, the appraiser rates each KRA on the appraisal directly at assessment, informed by whatever period evidence exists. Goals remain available as working records even in manual cycles; they simply do not compute the score. The rating still answers to the weightages: the KRA table's 100 points distribute the rated scores identically.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Self appraisal and Employee Performance Feedback", "12", "<p>Two structured inputs join the goal scores at the assessment window.</p><p><b>1. The self appraisal.</b> On the Appraisal record, the employee completes the self-appraisal section by the published date:</p><p>1. <b>The self ratings</b> — the employee rates the KRAs (or the configured self-assessment rows) from their own view of the period.<br>2. <b>The reflections</b> — the free-text account: what was delivered, what blocked, what support is asked for. Instruct staff to write to the evidence — deliveries and dates — because the self appraisal is read beside the goal records and the feedback, and agreement between the three is what makes the final conversation short.</p><p>The self appraisal's weight in the final score is the configuration's setting (Chapter 6); its value is independent of its weight — the reflections are the employee's documented voice in their own assessment, and the outcome conversation (Chapter 7) starts from them.</p><p><b>2. The Employee Performance Feedback.</b> Open Employee Performance Feedback (awesome bar) &gt; New — or from the appraisal's feedback section. One record per reviewer per appraisal:</p><p>• <b>The appraisal link</b> and the <b>reviewer</b> — the manager per the reporting structure (module 1's Reports To), plus additional named reviewers where the policy uses them (a second reviewer for calibration, a cross-functional reviewer where the role serves another function).<br>• <b>The rating rows</b> — the template's Rating Criteria rendered as rating fields: every reviewer rates the same criteria (Chapter 2's structured-comparison design). Ratings follow the site's scale; anchor the scale in the policy (what a 3 means, what a 5 means) so two reviewers' numbers mean the same thing.<br>• <b>The feedback text</b> — the reviewer's account, written to observations: what was done, when, with what result. The recruitment module's rule restated: observations, not speculation.</p><p><b>3. Feedback discipline.</b></p><p>1. Feedback is submitted by the published date; the completion read (Chapter 7) lists the overdue by reviewer, and an assessment window that closes with missing feedback scores people on partial inputs — the read exists so it does not.<br>2. Each reviewer submits independently; calibration discussions happen at the calibration read (Chapter 7), on the submitted records, not before them.<br>3. Feedback is confidential per the policy's disclosure rule: the employee sees what the policy says they see, consistently for everyone. Decide the rule once, publish it with the cycle calendar, and configure access accordingly — the sensitive-data handling rules from module 1 apply to appraisal content throughout.</p><p><b>4. The period-long alternative.</b> Where the site enables continuous feedback (feedback records created during the period, not only at the window), the same record and rules carry it; the assessment window then reads an accumulated file instead of a single retrospective. Use per the written policy; the mechanics do not change.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The final score", "12", "<p>The Appraisal record computes a final score from its inputs. This chapter covers the computation, its verification, and its handling.</p><p><b>1. The components.</b> Per the cycle's configuration (Chapter 3, step 4), the final score combines:</p><p>1. <b>The goal/KRA score</b> — automated from goal progress, or manually rated, per the cycle's method (Chapter 4), weighted by the template's KRA weightages.<br>2. <b>The self appraisal score</b> — the employee's self ratings, at the configured share.<br>3. <b>The feedback score</b> — the reviewers' ratings averaged across the submitted Employee Performance Feedback records, at the configured share.</p><p>The shares are the policy's decision, published with the cycle calendar. The computation is visible on the appraisal: each component, its figure, its share, the total.</p><p><b>2. Verification, per appraisal, before scores are released.</b></p><p>1. Every component present: goals reviewed and confirmed (Chapter 4, step 4), self appraisal submitted, all named reviewers' feedback submitted. An appraisal scored on missing components is flagged, not released — the completeness rule.<br>2. The arithmetic spot-checked on a sample: components × shares = the final figure shown.<br>3. The template correct for the role (the Chapter 3 generation spot-check, re-run on any appraisal whose score is disputed).</p><p><b>3. What the score is and is not.</b> Restating the Chapter 1 boundary as operating rules:</p><p>1. The score is a <b>summary of recorded inputs</b> — as good as the goals, ratings, and feedback behind it, and no better. The reads in Chapter 7 protect the inputs; nothing rescues a score built on stale goals and one reviewer's late guess.<br>2. The score <b>informs decisions made elsewhere</b>: the pay review reads it beside the movement record and the budget (the Payroll track, module 6); the promotion case reads it with the lifecycle documents (module 7). No lane in this module changes pay.<br>3. Score changes are governed: a disputed score is re-examined through its components — a goal's evidence, a rating's basis — and corrected through the records, never edited at the total. The appraisal's history (track changes per the standing audit rules) records any correction with its reason.</p><p><b>4. The outcome record.</b> When the cycle's scores are final:</p><p>1. Each appraisal is submitted per document rules — the period's assessment closed as a record.<br>2. The outcome conversation (Chapter 7) is held and its summary noted on the appraisal: what was discussed, what was agreed, the training needs identified (module 6 reads these).<br>3. The cycle's decision pack (Chapter 7's distribution read plus the individual records) goes to the deciders for whatever the policy connects to the round — with every number traceable to its inputs.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The cycle reads and administration", "12", "<p>The reads that keep a cycle honest and complete, and the administration around them.</p><p><b>1. The completion read (weekly during the assessment window).</b> From the cycle's appraisal list:</p><p>1. Self appraisals submitted versus total, overdue names listed.<br>2. Feedback submitted versus expected (appraisals × named reviewers), overdue by reviewer — the reviewer with eight overdue feedbacks is the bottleneck the read names while there is time to act.<br>3. Goals confirmed versus total (cycle start) and reviewed versus total (assessment window).</p><p>The read goes to HR's cycle owner and the responsible managers as a list; the cycle closes complete because the gaps were chased weekly, not discovered at the deadline.</p><p><b>2. The goal-freshness read (monthly during the period).</b> Goals with no progress update in the month, by employee and manager — data to the managers per the standing rule. In automated cycles this read protects the scores directly; in manual cycles it protects the assessment's evidence.</p><p><b>3. The calibration read (before scores release).</b> The draft score distribution by manager and by department:</p><p>1. A manager whose ratings sit far above or below the company distribution is a calibration conversation — about anchoring (what a 3 means), held on the records, before release.<br>2. The distribution itself is management data: presented as figures per the data-not-editorial rule; what the business does about its shape is the policy owner's decision.<br>3. Calibration adjusts <b>ratings through their raters</b> — a reviewer persuaded their anchoring was off revises their own feedback record with the change tracked. Nobody edits another's ratings.</p><p><b>4. The outcome conversation.</b> Per appraisee, after scores are final: the manager and employee walk the record — goals, self appraisal, feedback, score — and agree the notes: outcomes, development actions, training needs. The conversation's summary is entered on the appraisal (Chapter 6, step 4). The training needs entered here are module 6's intake: a low-scored KRA with an agreed development action becomes a training-plan line.</p><p><b>5. Administration rules.</b></p><p>1. <b>The cycle is scheduled, not improvised:</b> the calendar published at start (Chapter 3), the reads on their cadence, the close on its date. An assessment round run by reminder-and-hope produces the missing-component scores Chapter 6 refuses.<br>2. <b>Probation reviews are cycles:</b> a probation confirmation runs as a small cycle (the probationers as appraisees, the probation template, the confirmation decision informed by the score) — the same machinery at smaller scale, and the confirmation date field (module 1) completed from its outcome.<br>3. <b>Appraisal content is sensitive:</b> access per the policy's disclosure rule, consistently; the permission matrix covers the appraisal doctypes; the confidentiality rules from the payroll track's slip handling apply in kind.<br>4. <b>History stands:</b> past cycles' appraisals are never revised to match later opinions; a correction has a cause and a tracked change; templates revise forward (Chapter 2).</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the HealthTrade annual cycle", "13", "<p>This chapter applies Chapters 2-7 to HealthTrade. Configuration values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — templates (Chapter 2).</b> Three Appraisal Templates built with the managers and confirmed by the MD:</p><p>• <i>Branch Retail Staff</i> — KRAs: Dispensing accuracy 35, Customer service 25, Stock control 25, Policy compliance 15. Criteria: Reliability, Communication, Compliance.<br>• <i>Warehouse Operations</i> — KRAs: Stock accuracy 35, Dispatch timeliness 30, Safety compliance 20, Housekeeping 15.<br>• <i>Management</i> — KRAs: Branch performance 40, Team development 25, Controls compliance 20, Reporting timeliness 15.</p><p>Weightage totals validate at 100; decision references recorded on each template.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the cycle (Chapter 3).</b> Appraisal Cycle <i>2026 Annual</i> created for the calendar year; thirty appraisees loaded by department filters and mapped to their templates. KRA evaluation method: <b>manual rating</b> for this first cycle — recorded on the cycle with the reason (goal discipline not yet established; the honest-records rule) and the intention to move to automated scoring next cycle once the goal rhythm holds. The final score shares confirmed and published with the cycle calendar: KRA rating 60, feedback 25, self appraisal 15.</p><p><b>Step 3 — goals anyway (Chapter 4).</b> Goals are set at cycle start as working records even though this cycle scores manually: each appraisee drafts goals per KRA; managers confirm. Verification: every appraisee carries at least one confirmed goal per KRA. The monthly freshness read starts; month two lists nine stale goals across two branches — handed to the two managers as a list; month three lists two.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the assessment window (Chapters 5-6).</b> The window opens on the calendar's date. The weekly completion read runs: week one — self appraisals 18/30, feedback 12/60 (two reviewers per appraisal: the manager and one second reviewer for calibration); week two — the read names one reviewer holding eight overdue feedbacks; the cycle owner escalates and the feedbacks arrive within the week; window close — 30/30 self appraisals, 60/60 feedbacks, all KRA ratings entered. Score verification: components complete on all thirty; the arithmetic sampled on five appraisals clean.</p><p><b>Step 5 — calibration and release (Chapter 7).</b> The calibration read shows one branch's ratings averaging a full point above the company distribution; the anchoring conversation is held on the records, and the branch's second reviewer revises four of their own ratings with tracked changes and reasons. Scores release; outcome conversations run over two weeks; summaries and agreed actions entered on each appraisal.</p><p><b>Step 6 — the outputs.</b> The decision pack to the MD: the distribution, the individual records. Seven training needs identified in the outcome notes (five on stock-control KRAs, two on reporting) — entered as module 6's training-plan intake. The pay review round that follows reads the scores beside the movement record per the Payroll track's procedure; every pay change that results travels that track's lanes. The cycle's close note records the method decision for next year: goal discipline held from month three, automated KRA scoring approved for the 2027 cycle.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in performance management, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — templates without decisions.</b> Symptom: equal-split weightages nobody chose; KRAs copied between unrelated roles; scores that answer to no stated priority. Correction: the KRAs and weightages redrafted with the role's manager and confirmed; the revision applied to the next cycle. Prevention: the template design procedure — manager drafts, owner confirms weightages, decision reference recorded.</p><p><b>Error 2 — automated scores over dead goals.</b> Symptom: KRA scores computed from goals last updated at cycle start; assessment-week progress surges to 100%; scores nobody believes. Correction: the manager's evidence review before scores stand; unverifiable claims returned. Prevention: the honest method choice at cycle configuration and the monthly freshness read — automated scoring is adopted when the goal rhythm holds, not before.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the missing-component score.</b> Symptom: finals computed with absent feedback or unsubmitted self appraisals; people scored on partial inputs. Correction: the missing components obtained and the score recomputed. Prevention: the weekly completion read with overdue names, and the completeness rule — flagged, not released.</p><p><b>Error 4 — uncalibrated raters.</b> Symptom: one manager's team scoring systematically high or low; cross-team comparisons meaningless; the distribution driven by rater habits. Correction: the anchoring conversation on the records; raters revising their own ratings with tracked changes. Prevention: the scale anchored in the policy, and the calibration read before every release.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the score edited at the total.</b> Symptom: final figures adjusted directly to match a desired outcome; components no longer explaining the total. Correction: the edit reversed; the disputed score re-examined through its components. Prevention: the rule that corrections travel through the records — a goal's evidence, a rating's basis — and the tracked history on the appraisal.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the appraisal executing pay.</b> Symptom: pay changed on the appraisal conversation without the approval matrix, the letter, or the assignment; the payroll track's naked-assignment sweep catching the result. Correction: the change re-documented through the payroll lanes. Prevention: the Chapter 1 boundary operated — the score informs; the decision lanes execute.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the five record types and the pay boundary; build an Appraisal Template with weighted KRAs totalling 100 and rating criteria, through the design procedure; create an Appraisal Cycle with appraisee-template mapping, the evaluation method chosen honestly, and the published calendar; operate Goals — verifiable objectives per KRA, confirmed, fresh, evidence-reviewed; process self appraisals and Employee Performance Feedback with independent submission and the disclosure rule; verify and release final scores on complete components; and run the completion, freshness, and calibration reads with the outcome conversation.</p><p><b>Module rule 5: performance is assessed on records — templates weighted by decision, cycles run on a published calendar, scores computed from confirmed goals and submitted feedback, calibrated through the raters, and handed to the decision lanes as information, never as execution.</b> Module 6 covers training and development.</p>"),
]

